= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Apply the <chain rule> twice: once to the square and once to the argument $x^3$ of the <sine>. The inner <derivative> is $y'=3x^2\cos(x^3)-2\sin(2x)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{d(y^2)}{dx}=2\bigl[\sin(x^3)+\cos(2x)\bigr]\bigl[3x^2\cos(x^3)-2\sin(2x)\bigr].}
$$
The original PDF has $\sin(x^3)$, rather than the cube of $\sin x$.

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Find two numbers with sum $-1$ and product $-2$: they are $-2$ and $1$. Thus the <quadratic polynomial> factorizes as
$$
\boxed{x^2-x-2=(x-2)(x+1).}
$$
The product is negative precisely when its two factors have opposite signs. This occurs between its two <polynomial roots>, so
$$
\boxed{-1<x<2.}
$$
The endpoints give zero and are excluded by the strict inequality.

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Using the <Pythagorean trigonometric identity> and the <double-angle formula>,
$$
\sin^4\theta+\cos^4\theta=(\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta)^2-2\sin^2\theta\cos^2\theta=1-\frac12\sin^2(2\theta).
$$
The required value $1/2$ therefore occurs when $\sin^2(2\theta)=1$. Within $0\le\theta\le\pi$, the only possibilities are $2\theta=\pi/2$ and $3\pi/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\theta=\frac\pi4\quad\text{or}\quad\frac{3\pi}4.}
$$
Equivalently equality requires $\sin^2\theta=\cos^2\theta=1/2$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Both sums are <geometric series>. For first term $a$ and common ratio $r$ with $|r|<1$, the sum is $a/(1-r)$. Taking the initial term to be $2$ gives
$$
\boxed{2+1+\frac12+\frac14+\cdots=\frac2{1-1/2}=4,}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{2-1+\frac12-\frac14+\cdots=\frac2{1+1/2}=\frac43.}
$$
Both have <absolute convergence> because the series of absolute values has ratio $1/2$.

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

For $f(x)=xe^{-2x^2}$, the <product rule> and <chain rule> give
$$
f'(x)=e^{-2x^2}(1-4x^2).
$$
The positive exponential factor makes $f$ increasing for $0<x<1/2$ and decreasing for $1/2<x<1$. Its only interior <stationary point> is therefore a maximum at $x=1/2$. Checking it together with both endpoints,
$$
f(0)=0,\qquad f(1/2)=\frac12e^{-1/2},\qquad f(1)=e^{-2}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\min_{[0,1]}f=0\text{ at }x=0,\qquad\max_{[0,1]}f=\frac1{2\sqrt e}\text{ at }x=\frac12.}
$$
Checking the endpoints is necessary for extrema on a <closed interval>.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Both segments start at $(1,1,1)$, so their direction <vectors> are $\mathbf a=(-1,-1,-1)$ and $\mathbf b=(1,1,0)$. Their <dot product> is $-2$, and their <norms> are $\sqrt3$ and $\sqrt2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\cos\theta=\frac{\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b}{|\mathbf a||\mathbf b|}=-\frac2{\sqrt6}=-\sqrt{\frac23}.}
$$
The angle between the rays lies in $[0,\pi]$, so its <sine> is nonnegative:
$$
\boxed{\sin\theta=\sqrt{1-\cos^2\theta}=\frac1{\sqrt3}.}
$$
The negative <cosine> correctly identifies an obtuse angle. One also obtains the <sine> from $|\mathbf a\times\mathbf b|/(|\mathbf a||\mathbf b|)=\sqrt2/\sqrt6$, using the <cross product>.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

Let $p(x)=x^3-5x-1$. Its values at useful points are
$$
p(-3)=-13,\quad p(-2)=1,\quad p(-1)=3,\quad p(0)=-1,\quad p(2)=-3,\quad p(3)=11.
$$
Since a <polynomial> is a <continuous function>, the <intermediate value theorem> gives one <polynomial root> in each of the disjoint intervals $(-3,-2)$, $(-1,0)$ and $(2,3)$. A nonzero <cubic polynomial> has at most three distinct <polynomial roots>. Hence it has
$$
\boxed{\text{exactly three distinct real roots}.}
$$
These three distinct <polynomial roots> already exhaust its <degree of a polynomial>, so each has <multiplicity of a root> one.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Use <integration by substitution> with $u=x^4$, so $du=4x^3\,dx$. Then
$$
\boxed{\int x^3e^{-x^4}\,dx=\frac14\int e^{-u}\,du=-\frac14e^{-x^4}+C.}
$$
Differentiating the answer by the <chain rule> gives $x^3e^{-x^4}$, confirming the <antiderivative>. The real <constant of integration> $C$ is arbitrary.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The <tangent half-angle substitution> gives $\sin\theta=2t/(1+t^2)$ and $\cos\theta=(1-t^2)/(1+t^2)$. Dividing and then taking the reciprocal square of the <cosine> gives
$$
\boxed{\tan\theta=\frac{2t}{1-t^2},\qquad\sec^2\theta=\frac{(1+t^2)^2}{(1-t^2)^2}.}
$$
These formulas apply where $t$ is defined and $t\ne\pm1$; at $t=\pm1$ the original <tangent> and <secant function> are undefined because $\cos\theta=0$.

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

By <implicit differentiation> of the <ellipse> equation,
$$
18x+32y\frac{dy}{dx}=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{(1,1)}=-\frac9{16}.
$$
The <tangent line> through $(1,1)$ is therefore
$$
\boxed{y-1=-\frac9{16}(x-1),\qquad\text{equivalently }9x+16y=25.}
$$
Its <normal vector> is $(9,16)$, parallel to the <gradient> $(18,32)$ of $9x^2+16y^2$ at that point.

