= Solution
{parent=i}

Expand the square using $i^2=-1$:
$$
\left(\frac12+2i\right)^2=\frac14+2i-4=-\frac{15}4+2i.
$$
Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=-\frac{15}4,\qquad\operatorname{Im}=2.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Square the result of part (a)(i), keeping the cross term:
$$
\left(\frac12+2i\right)^4=\left(-\frac{15}4+2i\right)^2=\frac{225}{16}-4-15i=\frac{161}{16}-15i.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=\frac{161}{16},\qquad\operatorname{Im}=-15.}
$$

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $z=X+iY$. Its coordinates are $X=t$, $Y=1/t$, so $XY=1$. For the given positive range of $t$, this traces a segment of the first-quadrant <hyperbola>, moving rightward and downward:
$$
\boxed{z:\ (1/2,2)\longrightarrow(2,1/2).}
$$
The <complex conjugate> is $z^*=t-i/t$. It is the reflection in the <real axis>, tracing $XY=-1$ in the fourth quadrant, rightward and upward:
$$
\boxed{z^*:\ (1/2,-2)\longrightarrow(2,-1/2).}
$$
Neither trajectory crosses either axis, because $t$ and $1/t$ remain nonzero. The left panel of the figure marks the endpoints and directions.

\Image[paper-1-q11-complex-trajectories.png]
{title=Trajectories under <complex conjugation>, squaring and fourth powers, with endpoints and axis crossings}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Squaring gives
$$
u=z^2=t^2-t^{-2}+2i.
$$
Thus $\operatorname{Im}u=2$ is constant, while $d(\operatorname{Re}u)/dt=2t+2t^{-3}>0$. The trajectory is the horizontal line segment
$$
\boxed{u:\ (-15/4,2)\longrightarrow(15/4,2),\qquad\operatorname{Im}u=2.}
$$
It crosses the <imaginary axis> at $\boxed{(0,2)}$, when $t=1$, and never crosses the <real axis>. The middle panel shows the rightward direction.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $s=t^2-t^{-2}$. Part (b)(ii) gives $u=s+2i$, so
$$
v=u^2=(s^2-4)+4si.
$$
The parameter $s$ increases strictly from $-15/4$ to $15/4$. Therefore the <imaginary part> $Y=4s$ increases from $-15$ to $15$, while the <real part> is $X=s^2-4$. The endpoints are
$$
\boxed{v:\ (161/16,-15)\longrightarrow(161/16,15).}
$$
The <real axis> crossing is at $s=0$, giving $\boxed{(-4,0)}$ at $t=1$. The two <imaginary axis> crossings satisfy $s^2=4$, giving
$$
\boxed{(0,-8)\text{ at }t=\sqrt{\sqrt2-1},\qquad(0,8)\text{ at }t=\sqrt{\sqrt2+1}.}
$$
In order, the trajectory passes from its lower endpoint through $(0,-8)$, then the leftmost point $(-4,0)$, then $(0,8)$, to its upper endpoint. It is a right-opening <parabola> segment, traversed continuously upward as shown in the right panel.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

From $X=s^2-4$ and $Y=4s$, elimination of $s$ gives
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}v=\frac{(\operatorname{Im}v)^2}{16}-4,\qquad-15\le\operatorname{Im}v\le15.}
$$
This makes the <real part> a <quadratic function> of the <imaginary part>. There is no sign ambiguity because $Y$ determines $s=Y/4$ uniquely. The calculation illustrates <power maps of a reciprocal-coordinate hyperbola>.

