The <homogeneous linear differential equation> is $y''+y=0$. Its <characteristic equation> is $\lambda^2+1=0$, with roots $\pm i$. The general real <complementary function> is therefore
$$
\boxed{y_h(x)=C_1\cos x+C_2\sin x.}
$$
The two trigonometric solutions are linearly independent, supplying the two arbitrary constants required for this second-order <linear ordinary differential equation>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <method of undetermined coefficients>, choosing a <particular solution> $y_p=A+B\cos2x$. Substitution gives
$$
y_p''+y_p=A-3B\cos2x.
$$
Matching the constant and <cosine> coefficients to $\tfrac12-\tfrac12\cos2x$ gives $A=1/2$, $B=1/6$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y_p(x)=\frac12+\frac16\cos2x.}
$$
Neither forcing term is resonant with the homogeneous <angular frequency> one.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Combine the <complementary function> and <particular solution>:
$$
y=C_1\cos x+C_2\sin x+\frac12+\frac16\cos2x.
$$
The <boundary condition> at zero gives $C_1+2/3=0$, while that at $\pi/2$ gives $C_2+1/3=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=-\frac23\cos x-\frac13\sin x+\frac12+\frac16\cos2x.}
$$
The two <boundary conditions> determine both constants uniquely; substitution at each endpoint gives zero.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

First retain the equilibrium solution $\boxed{y\equiv0}$. Any nonzero real solution has constant sign on its interval of existence, since the polynomial right-hand side is <locally Lipschitz> and <Picard-Lindelöf theorem> prevents crossing the zero solution.

For a nonzero solution, the <Bernoulli differential equation> substitution $u=y^{-4}$ gives
$$
u'=-4y^{-5}y'=-8u-16.
$$
This <linear ordinary differential equation> has solution $u+2=Ce^{-8x}$. Therefore every nonzero real solution is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\pm\bigl(Ce^{-8x}-2\bigr)^{-1/4},\qquad C>0,\quad x<\frac18\ln\frac C2.}
$$
Either sign may be chosen and remains fixed. The restriction ensures $y^{-4}>0$; nonpositive $C$ gives no nonzero real solution. These are maximal real intervals, and their restrictions are also solutions. As the right endpoint is approached, $|y|$ diverges in <finite-time blowup>. The substitution would have lost $y=0$ if it had not been checked separately.

