= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <force> magnitude in <Newton's law of universal gravitation>, the <total differential> is
$$
\boxed{dF=\frac{Gm_2}{r^2}\,dm_1+\frac{Gm_1}{r^2}\,dm_2-\frac{2Gm_1m_2}{r^3}\,dr.}
$$
Dividing by the positive value $F$ gives the particularly useful relative form
$$
\boxed{\frac{dF}{F}=\frac{dm_1}{m_1}+\frac{dm_2}{m_2}-2\frac{dr}{r}.}
$$
For finite small changes this gives the first-order approximation to the fractional change; higher-order products of increments are omitted.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $dF=0$ in the relative <total differential> and use the two equal $1\%$ mass increases:
$$
0=0.01+0.01-2\frac{dr}{r}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\boxed{\frac{dr}{r}=0.01.}
$$
Thus the separation must **increase by $1\%$**. In this special case the result is also exact: the <mass> product increases by $1.01^2$, and preserving the inverse-square <force> requires $r_{\rm new}^2=1.01^2r^2$, hence $r_{\rm new}=1.01r$.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The original PDF specifies evaluation at $(1,1,1)$; the local TeX incorrectly gives $(1,1,0)$. Write the scalar field explicitly as $f=c/(x^2+y^2+z^2)$. Its <gradient> is
$$
\nabla f=-\frac{2c(x,y,z)}{(x^2+y^2+z^2)^2},\qquad\nabla f(1,1,1)=-\frac{2c}{9}(1,1,1).
$$
A <directional derivative> measures rate per unit distance, so normalize the direction to the <unit vector> $\mathbf n=(1,1,0)/\sqrt2$. Then
$$
\boxed{D_{\mathbf n}f(1,1,1)=\nabla f(1,1,1)\cdot\mathbf n=-\frac{2\sqrt2\,c}{9}.}
$$
Using the unnormalized direction vector would instead differentiate with respect to a path parameter, not distance, and would give a different scale.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The polar-coordinate <Jacobian matrix> and its <Jacobian determinant> are
$$
\frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(r,\theta)}=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta&-r\sin\theta\\\sin\theta&r\cos\theta\end{pmatrix},\qquad\boxed{J=r\cos^2\theta+r\sin^2\theta=r.}
$$
For $r>0$, the corresponding <area element> is $dx\,dy=r\,dr\,d\theta$. The determinant vanishes at $r=0$, where the polar angle does not give a unique coordinate representation.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Put $D=\sigma^2>0$ and $\xi=x-x_0$. Logarithmic <differentiation> of the positive <Gaussian function> gives
$$
\phi_t=\left(-\frac1{2t}+\frac{\xi^2}{4Dt^2}\right)\phi,\qquad\phi_x=-\frac{\xi}{2Dt}\phi.
$$
Differentiating once more in space gives
$$
\phi_{xx}=\left(-\frac1{2Dt}+\frac{\xi^2}{4D^2t^2}\right)\phi.
$$
Multiplying by $D$ proves
$$
\boxed{\phi_t=D\phi_{xx}=\sigma^2\phi_{xx}\qquad(t>0).}
$$
The assumption $\sigma\ne0$ ensures both the normalization and exponential denominator are defined. This is the <heat kernel>, with <diffusion coefficient> $D$ and centre $x_0$.

