= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume the three standard dice are fair and <independent>. For one die $X$, a <discrete uniform distribution> on $1,\ldots,6$ gives
$$
\mathbb EX=\frac{1+2+3+4+5+6}{6}=\frac72,\qquad\mathbb EX^2=\frac{1+4+9+16+25+36}{6}=\frac{91}6.
$$
The <variance> is $\operatorname{Var}X=\mathbb EX^2-(\mathbb EX)^2=35/12$. For the sum $Q=X_1+X_2+X_3$, <linearity of expectation> and addition of <variances> for <independent random variables> give
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EQ=\frac{21}2,\qquad\operatorname{Var}Q=3\frac{35}{12}=\frac{35}4.}
$$

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <sample space> has $6^3=216$ equally likely ordered outcomes. The repeated value can be any of the six faces, giving six outcomes $(j,j,j)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P(S)=\frac6{216}=\frac1{36}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose the first face in six ways, the second in five ways avoiding the first, and the third in four ways avoiding both. Thus
$$
\boxed{P(D)=\frac{6\cdot5\cdot4}{216}=\frac59.}
$$
Each ordered outcome is equally likely in this <sample space>, so dividing the count by $216$ gives the <probability>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

To find the <probability> of exactly one pair, choose its repeated face in six ways, the distinct face in five ways, and which of the three dice has that distinct face in three ways. Hence
$$
\boxed{P(T)=\frac{6\cdot5\cdot3}{216}=\frac5{12}.}
$$
As a check, the disjoint events $S,D,T$ exhaust the sample space, and $1/36+5/9+5/12=1$.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <sample space> outcomes with qualifying sums have totals three, four and five. Their ordered patterns are $(1,1,1)$, the three permutations of $(1,1,2)$, and the three permutations each of $(1,1,3)$ and $(1,2,2)$. Thus there are $1+3+3+3=10$ qualifying outcomes:
$$
\boxed{P(F)=\frac{10}{216}=\frac5{108}.}
$$

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Of the ten outcomes in $F$, all except $(1,1,1)$ have exactly one repeated pair. Hence $P(T\mid F)=9/10$. Applying <Bayes' theorem> in the requested direction,
$$
\boxed{P(F\mid T)=\frac{P(T\mid F)P(F)}{P(T)}=\frac{(9/10)(10/216)}{90/216}=\frac1{10}.}
$$
Equivalently there are nine favorable outcomes among the ninety outcomes in $T$. This direct count confirms the <conditional probability> calculation.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <fundamental theorem of calculus>, using continuity of $f$ near $x$, gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{dF}{dx}=f(x).}
$$
The lower endpoint $a$ is constant, so it contributes no additional term.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On the negative half-line, <differentiation> gives $d\ln(-u)/du=1/u$. The integration path from $-1$ to $x<0$ stays away from the singularity at zero. Therefore
$$
\boxed{F(x)=\int_{-1}^{x}\frac{du}{u}=\ln(-x)-\ln1=\ln(-x),\qquad x<0.}
$$
This choice satisfies $F(-1)=0$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A suitable real <antiderivative> on the disconnected domain is
$$
\boxed{G(x)=\ln|x|,\qquad x\ne0.}
$$
For $x>0$, $G'=1/x$ directly; for $x<0$, $G=\ln(-x)$ and the <chain rule> again gives $G'=1/x$. The graph is even, crosses zero at $x=\pm1$, tends to $-\infty$ on either side of zero, and tends to $+\infty$ as $|x|\to\infty$. Independent additive constants may be chosen on the two disconnected half-lines; both are set to zero here.

\Image[paper-1-q17-antiderivative-branches.png]
{title=Real <antiderivative> branches for $1/x$ and $1/\sqrt{x^2-1}$, with their domains and limiting behavior}

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

At $u=1$, the integrand is unbounded but integrable: $1/\sqrt{u^2-1}\sim[2(u-1)]^{-1/2}$. Thus the lower-limit integral is understood as an <improper integral>. With $z=u+\sqrt{u^2-1}$,
$$
\frac{dz}{du}=1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{u^2-1}}=\frac z{\sqrt{u^2-1}},\qquad\frac{du}{\sqrt{u^2-1}}=\frac{dz}{z}.
$$
The lower endpoint corresponds to $z=1$, giving
$$
\boxed{F(x)=\ln\left(x+\sqrt{x^2-1}\right)=\operatorname{arcosh}x,\qquad x>1.}
$$
The limiting value at $x\downarrow1$ is zero. Here $\operatorname{arcosh}$ is the <inverse hyperbolic cosine>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A single formula valid on both real components is
$$
\boxed{R(x)=\ln\left|x+\sqrt{x^2-1}\right|,\qquad |x|>1.}
$$
Let $s=\sqrt{x^2-1}>0$. Since $x+s\ne0$,
$$
R'(x)=\frac{1+x/s}{x+s}=\frac1s=\frac1{\sqrt{x^2-1}}.
$$
Equivalently, $R(x)=\operatorname{arcosh}x$ for $x>1$ and $R(x)=-\operatorname{arcosh}(-x)$ for $x<-1$. The graph is odd for this choice of constants, increases on both components, and approaches zero with vertical tangent as $x\to1^+$ or $x\to-1^-$. Its far-field behaviors are $R(x)\sim\ln(2x)$ on the right and $R(x)\sim-\ln(-2x)$ on the left. The interval $[-1,1]$ is excluded. Independent additive constants could again be assigned to the two components. The right panel sketches these <real inverse-hyperbolic antiderivative branches>.

= d
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $H(u)=\sin^2u+\ln u$. The <chain rule> gives $H'(u)=\sin2u+1/u$, so the two given summands combine into its <logarithmic derivative>, $f(u)=H'(u)/H(u)$. For $u>1$, $H(u)>0$, so the real logarithm is defined throughout the integration path. Since $H(\pi)=\ln\pi$,
$$
\boxed{F(x)=\ln\left(\frac{\sin^2x+\ln x}{\ln\pi}\right),\qquad x>1.}
$$
This satisfies $F(\pi)=0$, and <differentiation> recovers both terms of the integrand.

= 18
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <matrix trace> is the sum of diagonal entries:
$$
\operatorname{Tr}M=a^2+1-1=a^2.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{a=\pm\sqrt3,\qquad b\in\mathbb R\text{ arbitrary}.}
$$
The first diagonal entry is $a^2$ in the original PDF.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Expanding the <determinant> along the first row gives
$$
\det M=-a^2-b^2+3b+3.
$$
Under the <matrix trace> condition $a^2=3$, this simplifies to $\det M=b(3-b)$. A square <matrix> is invertible exactly when its <determinant> is nonzero. Thus
$$
\boxed{a=\pm\sqrt3,\qquad b\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0,3\}.}
$$

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <characteristic polynomial> of $A$ is
$$
\det(A-\lambda I)=(-5-\lambda)(5-\lambda)+16=\lambda^2-9.
$$
Its distinct <eigenvalues> are $3$ and $-3$. For $3$, the <eigenvector> relation gives $y=-2x$, so choose $(1,-2)^T$. For $-3$, it gives $x=-2y$, so choose $(2,-1)^T$. Put these independent <eigenvectors> into columns:
$$
\boxed{B=\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\-2&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad C=\begin{pmatrix}3&0\\0&-3\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Then $AB=BC$ column by column. Also $\det B=3\ne0$, so this choice supplies the <diagonalization of a matrix> $B^{-1}AB=C$. Scaling columns or interchanging their order gives other valid choices, with the matching order in $C$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the choice of $B$ in part (b)(i), the two-by-two <matrix inverse> formula gives
$$
\boxed{B^{-1}=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}-1&-2\\2&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplication gives $BB^{-1}=B^{-1}B=I$, providing a direct check.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The expression assumes $Y$ is invertible. By the <cyclic property of the trace>,
$$
\operatorname{Tr}(YXY^{-1})=\operatorname{Tr}(XY^{-1}Y)=\operatorname{Tr}X.
$$
For a real or complex <antisymmetric matrix>, $X^T=-X$ forces every diagonal entry to satisfy $x_{ii}=-x_{ii}$, hence $x_{ii}=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Tr}Z=\operatorname{Tr}X=0.}
$$
This also demonstrates invariance of the <matrix trace> under <matrix similarity>. Antisymmetry of $Z$ itself is not needed.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An <orthogonal matrix> has orthonormal columns. The first column of $L$ has squared <norm> $1+c^2$, so $c=0$. Orthogonality of the two columns then gives $-b+cd=-b=0$, hence $b=0$. The second column must have squared <norm> $d^2=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{b=c=0,\qquad d=\pm1.}
$$
Both resulting matrices satisfy $L^TL=I$ directly.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Among the two <orthogonal matrices> from part (d)(i), $d=-1$ gives $L=-I$, a <rotation matrix> through angle $\pi$. For $d=1$, the <determinant> is $-1$, so the matrix reverses orientation and is a reflection rather than a rotation:
$$
\boxed{b=c=0,\qquad d=1,\qquad L=\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
It is the <reflection matrix> across the $y$ axis.

= e
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For any real <square matrix> $N$, the <symmetric and antisymmetric parts of a matrix> give $N=(N+N^T)/2+(N-N^T)/2$, whose summands are respectively a <symmetric matrix> and an <antisymmetric matrix>. Here it gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&3\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}a&(b+c)/2\\(b+c)/2&3\end{pmatrix}+\begin{pmatrix}0&(b-c)/2\\(c-b)/2&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Transposing the first summand leaves it unchanged; transposing the second changes its sign. Entrywise addition recovers the original <matrix>. The decomposition is unique because a matrix that is both symmetric and antisymmetric must be zero.

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Since $A>0$ and $\omega>0$ are fixed, the standard <sine> limit gives
$$
f(x)=A\omega\,\operatorname{sgn}(x)|x|^{p+1}\frac{\sin(\omega x)}{\omega x}\sim A\omega\,\operatorname{sgn}(x)|x|^{p+1}\qquad(x\to0).
$$
If $p>-1$, this tends to the assigned value zero. At $p=-1$, the right and left limits are $+A\omega$ and $-A\omega$; if $p<-1$, they diverge with opposite signs. Hence
$$
\boxed{f\text{ is continuous at }0\iff p>-1,\quad\text{for every allowed }A,\omega>0.}
$$
The cancellation comes from the simple zero of $\sin(\omega x)$ at the origin, not rapid oscillation there.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <derivative> definition and the specified value $f(0)=0$:
$$
\frac{f(h)-f(0)}h=A\omega|h|^p\frac{\sin(\omega h)}{\omega h}.
$$
The last factor tends to one. Therefore the limit is zero when $p>0$, equals $A\omega$ when $p=0$, and diverges to $+\infty$ when $p<0$. A finite <derivative> exists exactly when
$$
\boxed{p\ge0,\qquad f'(0)=\begin{cases}0,&p>0,\\A\omega,&p=0.\end{cases}}
$$
In particular $-1<p<0$ gives continuity without differentiability. The threshold is an example of <regularity of a power times a function with a simple zero>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For every positive integer $n$, the bound $|\sin(\omega n)|\le1$ gives
$$
|f(n)|=A n^p|\sin(\omega n)|\le A n^p.
$$
For $p<-1$, the positive <p-series> $\sum n^p$ converges. The <comparison test> therefore proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^\infty f(n)\text{ converges absolutely, and hence converges}.}
$$
No special arithmetic condition on $\omega$ is required for this sufficient condition.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Factor out the dominant cubic term and use a <geometric series> in $1/(3x^2)$:
$$
\frac1{x+3x^3}=\frac1{3x^3}\left(1+\frac1{3x^2}\right)^{-1}=\frac1{3x^3}-\frac1{9x^5}+O(x^{-7}).
$$
Thus the requested leading term and absolute error order are
$$
\boxed{g(x)=\frac1{3x^3}+O(x^{-5})\qquad(x\to+\infty).}
$$
The relative error of retaining only the leading term is $O(x^{-2})$; it differs from the absolute error order $O(x^{-5})$.

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $a=\pi/3$, $b=2\pi/3$ and take $n$ equal subintervals of width $h=(b-a)/n=\pi/(3n)$. Since <sine> is continuous, its integral is the limit of the left-endpoint <Riemann sums>
$$
J=\lim_{n\to\infty}h\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\sin(a+jh).
$$
Using <Euler's formula>, the corresponding complex sum is a finite <geometric series>:
$$
S_n=h\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}e^{i(a+jh)}=he^{ia}\frac{1-e^{inh}}{1-e^{ih}},\qquad nh=b-a=\frac\pi3.
$$
Since $(e^{ih}-1)/h\to i$,
$$
\lim S_n=\frac{e^{ib}-e^{ia}}i=\frac{(-1/2+i\sqrt3/2)-(1/2+i\sqrt3/2)}i=i.
$$
Taking <imaginary parts> gives
$$
\boxed{J=1.}
$$
One can see the same limit entirely within the real sums: the finite trigonometric-sum identity obtained from this geometric sum gives
$$
h\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\sin(a+jh)=h\frac{\sin(nh/2)}{\sin(h/2)}\sin\left(a+\frac{n-1}{2}h\right)=\frac h2\cot\frac h2\longrightarrow1.
$$
Thus the evaluation is explicitly by a <Riemann sum>, as required.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Apply the <Leibniz integral rule>: differentiate both moving endpoints and the integrand's explicit $x$ dependence. The <partial derivative> is $\partial_x\sin(xt^2)=t^2\cos(xt^2)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
f'(x)={}&\beta e^{\beta x}\sin\left(xe^{2\beta x}\right)-\alpha e^{\alpha x}\sin\left(xe^{2\alpha x}\right)\\
&+\int_{e^{\alpha x}}^{e^{\beta x}}t^2\cos(xt^2)\,dt.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The signs distinguish upper and lower endpoint contributions. The formula also holds when the limits occur in reversed order, by the oriented-integral convention. If $\alpha=\beta$, the integral defining $f$ is identically zero and all displayed terms cancel, so
$$
\boxed{f'(x)=0\quad\text{when }\alpha=\beta.}
$$

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

First check the <improper integral> exists: near zero its numerator is $(\beta-\alpha)x^2+O(x^4)$, so the quotient is $O(x)$; at infinity both exponentials decay. For positive parameters in compact neighborhoods, the differentiated integrands have integrable bounds, justifying <differentiation under the integral sign>. It gives
$$
\frac{\partial I}{\partial\alpha}=-\int_0^\infty xe^{-\alpha x^2}\,dx=-\frac1{2\alpha},\qquad\frac{\partial I}{\partial\beta}=\int_0^\infty xe^{-\beta x^2}\,dx=\frac1{2\beta}.
$$
Thus $I=-\tfrac12\ln\alpha+\tfrac12\ln\beta+C$, with $C$ independent of both parameters. Since $I(\alpha,\alpha)=0$, $C=0$, and
$$
\boxed{I(\alpha,\beta)=\frac12\ln\frac\beta\alpha.}
$$
The sign is consistent with the integrand: $\beta>\alpha$ makes it positive. Neither separate integral $\int_0^\infty e^{-\alpha x^2}\,dx/x$ converges at zero; it is the difference that is meaningful. The substitution $u=x^2$ identifies the result as a <Frullani integral> with an extra factor $1/2$.
